    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Piccadilly’s name comes from a 17th‑century tailor’s “pickadills,” stiff lace collars he sold.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.discoverbritain.com/destinations/london/history-piccadilly-london/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Shaftesbury Memorial Fountain at Piccadilly Circus honors the 7th Earl of Shaftesbury, a Victorian philanthropist.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.discoverbritain.com/destinations/london/history-piccadilly-london/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1908 Perrier installed the first bulb‑lit advertisement over the fountain, creating the bright hoardings that still catch the eye today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.discoverbritain.com/destinations/london/history-piccadilly-london/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the 1920s Piccadilly Circus had become a royal‑favoured street, bustling with theatres, dance halls and high‑profile visitors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.discoverbritain.com/destinations/london/history-piccadilly-london/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Piccadilly itself runs from Market Street to London Road, cutting across the heart of London’s city centre.",
